The main objective of this paper is to investigate multiple solution possibilities for a specific nonuniform Kirchhoff type elliptic system of equations that arise from the singular Trudenger-Moser inequality expansion. The system in question is given by the equation

\begin{align*}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\mu_1\Delta u=\lambda_1f(v)^p+\mu_1h_1(x),\quad x\in\Omega,\\
\mu_2\Delta v=\lambda_2f(u)^p+\mu_2h_2(x),\quad x\in\Omega,\\
u|_{\partial\Omega}=0,\quad v|_{\partial\Omega}=0,
\end{array}
\right.
\end{align*}

where $\Omega$ represents a subdomain of $\mathbb{R}^2$ with boundary $\partial\Omega$, and $\mu_1,\mu_2,\lambda_1,\lambda_2$ are nonnegative parameters with at least one of them being positive. The nonlinear term $f$ expands into a singular Trudenger-Moser inequality near zero, and is monotonically increasing for large inputs. A multiple solution existence theorem is established for this system, under the condition that its first variational mountain pass ingenuity is preserved. This result holds true when the domain $\Omega$ contains a neighborhood of the origin. If the origin does not lie within $\Omega$, a different set of functions satisfying the singular Trudenger-Moser inequality must be chosen, and an alternative multiple solution existence theorem is applicable. Consequently, this technique yields a novel proof for the existence of multiple solutions to a single equation, which is derived from the singular Trudenger-Moser inequality expansion. The novel approach presented in this paper may also be applied to modify and extend established results concerning another type of Kirchhoff type elliptic system. Additionally, this paper explores new potential applications and complications arising from the singular Trudenger-Moser inequality expansion, particularly in relation to the critical growth term, and identifies areas for future research and development. A discussion on the new issues and challenges that arise in the analysis of a Kirchhoff type elliptic system of equations is also provided in this paper. It identifies areas for further exploration and offers possible solutions to tackle some of the emerging difficulties. Finally, this paper extends and modifies earlier works on a single equation, which is derived from the singular Trudenger-Moser

